Rennari Client JS
=================

JavaScript client for Mojo::Rennari, a small MessagePack/JSON WebSocket
event framework for Mojolicious.

Wire-compatible with the Perl server. Works in Node 18+ and any modern
browser. No build step required.

STATUS

Working. The Perl server and this client are tested against each other.
See mojo-rennari for the server.

INSTALL

    npm install rennari-client-js

Or clone and import directly from src.

QUICK START

    import { Rennari } from 'rennari-client-js';

    const client = new Rennari('wss://example.com/ws?token=secret');

    client.on('connect', () => {
        console.log('connected, session', client.sessionId);
        client.emit('chat', { room: 'lobby', text: 'hi' });
    });

    client.on('chat', (payload) => {
        console.log(payload.text);
    });

    client.on('disconnect', ({ code, reason }) => {
        console.log('disconnected', code, reason);
    });

    client.on('ack_timeout', ({ event, payload }) => {
        console.warn('no ack for', event);
    });

    await client.connect();

CORE CONCEPTS

Frames

Every message is a map:

    { e, d, id?, o?, n? }

- e - event name
- d - payload
- id - optional acknowledgement id
- o - optional offset, present when the server has recovery enabled
- n - optional namespace, omitted for /

The client uses the same wire format as the Perl server. If the server
is configured with serializer: 'json', the client must be too:

    const client = new Rennari(url, { serializer: 'json' });

Events

    client.on(event, handler);
    client.off(event, handler);
    client.emit(event, payload);

Client-level events (on the default / namespace) are also the place where
local lifecycle events fire:

    client.on('connect', () => {});
    client.on('disconnect', ({ code, reason }) => {});
    client.on('error', (err) => {});
    client.on('ack_timeout', ({ event, payload }) => {});
    client.on('reconnect_attempt', ({ attempt, delay }) => {});
    client.on('reconnect_failed', () => {});
    client.on('recovered', ({ offset }) => {});

Namespaces

    const chat = client.of('/chat');

    chat.on('message', (payload) => {});
    chat.emit('message', { text: 'hi' });

Every non-default namespace is declared to the server on connect and on
reconnect. Incoming frames are dispatched by their n field, not by any
connection state. A frame for a namespace the client has not joined on
the server side causes a close with code 4005.

Acknowledgements

    const ack = await client.emitWithAck('chat', { text: 'hi' }, {
        timeout: 3000,
        retries: 2,
    });

The client assigns an id, sends the frame, and waits for rennari_ack.
If no ack arrives within timeout milliseconds, the frame is resent up to
retries times. When retries are exhausted, the promise rejects and the
ack_timeout local event fires.

Duplicate delivery is possible by design: retries resend the same frame
with the same id. Handlers on the server should be idempotent if the
application uses retries.

Reconnection

On by default:

    const client = new Rennari(url, {
        reconnection: true,
        reconnectionDelay: 1000,
        reconnectionDelayMax: 5000,
        reconnectionAttempts: Infinity,
        reconnectionJitter: 0.5,
    });

When the WebSocket closes unexpectedly, the client reconnects with
exponential backoff plus jitter. Before each attempt, reconnect_attempt
fires. When attempts are exhausted, reconnect_failed fires. On successful
reconnect, connect fires again.

While disconnected, emit() queues the frame in an outbox. The queue is
flushed on the next connect. If reconnection is off, emit() throws when
not connected.

Recovery

When the server has recovery enabled, it issues a session id at
handshake. The client stores it as client.sessionId and tracks the
server's offset as client.lastOffset.

If the connection drops and reconnects within the server's recovery
window (default 120 seconds), the client sends rennari_resume with the
session id and last offset. The server replays missed frames, re-attaches
namespaces and rooms, and replies rennari_resumed with the transferred
session id. The client then fires the recovered local event.

If the session has expired on the server, the server replies
rennari_resume_failed, the client clears its session state, and connects
fresh.

    const client = new Rennari(url, {
        recovery: true,
        recoveryWindow: 120000,
    });

Both recovery and reconnection must be enabled for session recovery to
work. Without reconnection, the client never tries to resume.

DISCONNECT

    client.disconnect();                  // 1000, 'client_disconnect'
    client.disconnect(4000, 'user_exit'); // custom

Intentional disconnect does not trigger reconnection.

CONFIGURATION

    new Rennari(url, {
        serializer: 'msgpack',          // or 'json'
        maxBytes: 64 * 1024,            // max incoming frame size
        ackTimeout: 5000,               // ms, default per emitWithAck
        retries: 3,                     // default per emitWithAck
        reconnection: true,
        reconnectionDelay: 1000,
        reconnectionDelayMax: 5000,
        reconnectionAttempts: Infinity,
        reconnectionJitter: 0.5,
        recovery: true,
        recoveryWindow: 120000,
    });

NODE VERSUS BROWSER

In the browser, globalThis.WebSocket exists. In Node 18 and 20, it does
not, so the ws package must be provided:

    import { WebSocket } from 'ws';
    import { setWebSocket, Rennari } from 'rennari-client-js';
    setWebSocket(WebSocket);

Node 22+ has a built-in WebSocket and does not need the ws package.

In both environments, binary WebSocket frames are used. binaryType is set
to 'arraybuffer' by the transport.

CLOSE CODES

The server closes with:

- 4002 - bad frame
- 4003 - unknown event
- 4004 - handler error
- 4005 - unknown namespace
- 4006 - recovery disabled but client sent rennari_resume

These arrive in the disconnect local event as { code, reason }.

EXAMPLES

A Node smoke test is in examples/node.mjs. It connects to a running Perl
server, exercises custom events, namespaces, acks, and recovery across a
forced disconnect.

    node examples/node.mjs <port>

Integration tests in tests/integration.test.js do the same thing
programmatically against a server spawned from the Perl repo:

    RENNARI_PERL_DIR=/path/to/mojo-rennari npm test

Tests are skipped if RENNARI_PERL_DIR is unset.

